Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","KP","Universities","University Colleges","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-1d3dd1bf-d219-469a-a052-985b5918d62f","kp-d72182a9-bbe2-47c5-94ca-105819e9ab57","ku-8aebb134-f123-4344-a102-5d6fee8d1177","sdu-c8d844e7-2f60-4447-9da6-70e89c4eefc6"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KP University College Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Contributions of mean and shape of blood pressure distribution to worldwide trends and variations in raised blood pressure : A pooled analysis of 1018 population-based measurement studies with 88.6 million participants","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-099.93-014723","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Commitment schemes are a fundamental primitive in modern cryptography, serving as a building block for a myriad of complex protocols and applications. Universally composable commitment schemes are of particular interest, since they can be seamlessly combined with other universally composable primitives and protocols while retaining security guarantees. Moreover, commitments with homomorphic properties enable significantly more efficient constructions of protocols for applications such as zero knowledge proofs, two-party computation through garbled circuits and multiparty computation. However, achieving universal composability for commitment schemes often sacrifices both concrete and asymptotic efficiency, specially if homomorphic properties are required. In this thesis we bridge the gap between stand alone and universally composable commitment schemes, for which we achieve optimal efficiency while obtaining homomorphic properties. We obtain schemes with amortized linear computational complexity and an optimal amortized ratio between message length and the amount of communication required to commit to a message, which we call \\emph{rate}. We also construct schemes with additive and/or multiplicative homomorphism. Our best scheme achieves linear complexity for both the sender and the receiver, rate-1 and additive homomorphism. On the other hand, schemes with both multiplicative and additive homomorphism based on our approach can only achieve poly-logarithmic overhead for both communication and computational complexity. We develop a technique for constructing universally composable commitments schemes that allow for an \\emph{unlimited} number of commitments using solely cheap symmetric key operations after a setup phase that requires only a small number of expensive public key operations completely independent from both the number of commitments to be executed later and their contents. The downside of achieving universal composability for most interesting two party and multiparty functionalities lies on the fact that some form of \\emph{setup} is required. We use a number of oblivious transfers only related to a statistical security parameter as a setup. The rest of our constructions leverage secret sharing and coding theory techniques, including a novel method for verifying that a large number of strings are codewords of a given linear code with linear complexity.","author_count":1,"contributors":["David, Bernardo Machado"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-275202"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Commitment","Information theory","Provable Security","Universal Composability","Verifiable Secret Sharing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a framework for efficient homomorphic universally composable commitments","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dtd","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/428987122/Bernardo_Machado_Davids_thesis.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-275202","ids":["au-ff9f5407-8e8e-438e-ae22-5de2d15cfbec"],"keyword":["Commitment","Information Theory","Provable Security","Universal Composability","Verifiable Secret Sharing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E58FEFE029814F9415A03CD6F2F90068"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/428987122/Bernardo_Machado_Davids_thesis.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bernardo Machado","key":"davidpau","last":"David","locid":"e39dd946-3171-4c2c-acc2-0990fe5230b3","name":"David, Bernardo Machado","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"106","place":"Aarhus, Denmark","publisher":"Department of Computer Science, University of Aarhus","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-ff9f5407-8e8e-438e-ae22-5de2d15cfbec"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"A Framework For Efficient Homomorphic Universally Composable Commitments","type":"Thesis Doctoral","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-275202","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"This paper presents efficient structure-preserving signature schemes based on simple assumptions such as decisional linear. We first give two general frameworks for constructing fully secure signature schemes from weaker building blocks such as variations of one-time signatures and random message secure signatures. They can be seen as refinements of the Even–Goldreich–Micali framework, and preserve many desirable properties of the underlying schemes such as constant signature size and structure preservation. We then instantiate them based on simple (i.e., not q-type) assumptions over symmetric and asymmetric bilinear groups. The resulting schemes are structure-preserving and yield constant-size signatures consisting of 11–14 group elements, which compares favorably to existing schemes whose security relies on q-type assumptions.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Abe, Masayuki","Chase, Melissa","David, Bernardo Machado","Kohlweiss, Markulf","Nishimaki, Ryo","Ohkubo, Miyako"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-227497"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Abe, Masayuki"],"country":["Japan","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Microsoft USA","Nippon Telegraph & Telephone"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"constant size structure preserving signatures generic constructions and simple assumptions","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84936817918","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-227497","identifier":[{"id":"84936817918","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-ac26eeda-3913-4466-ac07-9470777a3c9a"],"keyword":["Extended random message attacks","Partially one-time signatures","Structure-preserving signatures","Tagged one-time signatures"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0BA5B619DD4AD381E05EEB90197959DD"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"jp","key":"nippon telegraph telephone","loc":["Japan","Asia, East","OECD"],"name":"Nippon Telegraph & Telephone","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"microsoft usa","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Microsoft USA","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Masayuki","key":"abepau","last":"Abe","name":"Abe, Masayuki","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Melissa","key":"chasepau","last":"Chase","name":"Chase, Melissa","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Bernardo","key":"davidpau","last":"David","locid":"e39dd946-3171-4c2c-acc2-0990fe5230b3","name":"David, Bernardo Machado","name_org":"David, Bernardo","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Markulf","key":"kohlweisspau","last":"Kohlweiss","name":"Kohlweiss, Markulf","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ryo","key":"nishimakipau","last":"Nishimaki","name":"Nishimaki, Ryo","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Miyako","key":"ohkubopau","last":"Ohkubo","name":"Ohkubo, Miyako","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00145-015-9211-7","issn":["09332790","14321378"],"issn_org":["09332790"],"issue":"4","journal":"Journal of Cryptology","pages":"1-46","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Journal of Cryptology","volume":"29"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84936817918"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-ac26eeda-3913-4466-ac07-9470777a3c9a"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Constant-Size Structure-Preserving Signatures : Generic Constructions and Simple Assumptions","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-227497","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"We construct the first UC commitment scheme for binary strings with the optimal properties of rate approaching 1 and linear time complexity (in the amortised sense, using a small number of seed OTs). On top of this, the scheme is additively homomorphic, which allows for applications to maliciously secure 2-party computation. As tools for obtaining this, we make three contributions of independent interest: we construct the first (binary) linear time encodable codes with non-trivial distance and rate approaching 1, we construct the first almost universal hash function with small seed that can be computed in linear time, and we introduce a new primitive called interactive proximity testing that can be used to verify whether a string is close to a given linear code.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Cascudo, Ignacio","Damgård, Ivan","David, Bernardo Machado","Döttling, Nico","Nielsen, Jesper Buus"],"cuid":["S02-083.45-004111"],"event":{"acronym":"CRYPTO","end":"2016-08-18","place":"Santa Barbara, us","start":"2016-08-14","title":"36th Annual International Cryptology Conference, CRYPTO 2016","year":"2016"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["David, Bernardo Machado"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of California at Berkeley"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"rate 000000000001 linear time and additively homomorphic uc commitments","src":["aau","au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84979608655","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-083.45-004111","